SSH + GitHub + Local Machine Setup (Complete Notes)
======================================================
Use this as your personal checklist for a new laptop or new user.

1. Install Prerequisites

Verify:

git --version

py --version


Expected:

git version x.x.x
Python 3.13.x

2. Configure Git User

Check:

git config --global user.name

git config --global user.email


If not configured:

git config --global user.name "<username>"

git config --global user.email "<username>@gmail.com"


Verify:

git config --global --list

3. Create GitHub Repository

GitHub:

Profile
→ Repositories
→ New Repository


Example:

RF-BACKEND_AUTOMATION


Create empty repository.

Do NOT add:

README
.gitignore
License

4. Generate SSH Key

Open PowerShell:

ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "<username>@gmail.com"


When prompted:

Enter file in which to save the key:


Press:

Enter


Uses default:

C:\Users\<username>\.ssh\id_ed25519


When prompted:

Enter passphrase:


Option 1 (Recommended)

Enter your own passphrase


Option 2

Just press Enter twice


(no passphrase)

Files created:

id_ed25519
id_ed25519.pub

5. Add SSH Key To GitHub

Display public key:

Get-Content $HOME\.ssh\id_ed25519.pub


Copy entire output:

ssh-ed25519 AAAA.......


GitHub:

Profile
→ Settings
→ SSH and GPG Keys
→ New SSH Key


Title:

Capgemini Laptop 2026


Key:

Paste entire ssh-ed25519 line


Save.

6. Verify SSH Connection

Run:

ssh -T git@github.com


If:

The authenticity of host ...


type:

yes


If passphrase was configured:

Enter passphrase for key:


Type passphrase.

Success:

Hi <username>!
You've successfully authenticated,
but GitHub does not provide shell access.


SSH setup complete ✅

7. Create Local Repository

Create folder:

mkdir rf-backend-automation

cd rf-backend-automation


Initialize git:

git init


Rename branch:

git branch -M main

8. Connect Local Repo To GitHub

Add SSH remote:

git remote add origin git@github.com:<username>/RF-BACKEND_AUTOMATION.git


Verify:

git remote -v


Expected:

origin git@github.com:<username>/RF-BACKEND_AUTOMATION.git

9. Create Python Virtual Environment

Inside project:

py -3.13 -m venv .venv


Activate:

.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1


Verify:

python --version

python -m pip --version

10. Create .gitignore

Create:

.gitignore


Content:

.venv/

results/

output.xml
report.html
log.html

__pycache__/

.idea/
.vscode/


Important:

❌ Never commit .venv

11. Create requirements.txt
robotframework==7.4.2

robotframework-requests==0.9.7

robotframework-databaselibrary==2.4.1

robotframework-soaplibrary==1.4

robotframework-datadriver==1.11.2

robotframework-jsonlibrary==0.5

oracledb==3.4.2

pyyaml==6.0.2

numpy

pandas


Install:

python -m pip install -r requirements.txt

12. Verify Robot Framework
robot --version


Expected:

Robot Framework 7.x

13. Install VS Code Extensions

Install:

RobotCode - Robot Framework Support


Then:

Ctrl+Shift+P


Select:

Python: Select Interpreter


Choose:

rf-backend-automation\.venv\Scripts\python.exe

14. Commit To GitHub

Add files:

git add .


Commit:

git commit -m "Initial RF backend framework setup"


Push:

git push -u origin main


If asked:

Enter passphrase for key:


Enter SSH passphrase.

Refresh GitHub.

Repository now contains your framework.

Final RF Structure
rf-backend-automation
│
├── tests
│   ├── rest
│   ├── soap
│   └── db
│
├── resources
│   ├── common.resource
│   ├── rest_keywords.resource
│   ├── soap_keywords.resource
│   └── db_keywords.resource
│
├── config
│   ├── O2.yaml
│   ├── T2.yaml
│   ├── O3.yaml
│   ├── T3.yaml
│   ├── O6.yaml
│   └── T6.yaml
│
├── variables
│
├── results
│
├── requirements.txt
├── azure-pipelines.yml
├── README.md
├── .gitignore
└── .venv (NOT COMMITTED)

Golden Rules

✅ Commit:

.robot
.resource
.yaml
.py
requirements.txt
azure-pipelines.yml
README.md


❌ Never commit:

.venv
output.xml
report.html
log.html
results


✅ Use SSH:

git@github.com:<username>/RF-BACKEND_AUTOMATION.git


✅ Other developers only need:

git clone <repo>

py -3.13 -m venv .venv

.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1

pip install -r requirements.txt


then now you are ready to code. 🚀